(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-htn-alpha-central-vasodilators-quiz"]={"title":"Alpha-1 Blockers, Central Agents & Vasodilators Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which adverse effect is characteristic of oral minoxidil?","choices":["Lupus syndrome","Hypertrichosis","Cyanide toxicity","Dry cough"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oral minoxidil causes hair growth on the face, back, arms and legs; it also causes myocardial ischemia and arrhythmias. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Why do direct vasodilators lose their effect over time when used alone?","choices":["Reflex responses cause tachyphylaxis","They induce their own metabolism","They block their own receptor","They cause hypokalemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Arteriolar dilation triggers reflex sympathetic activation (tachycardia, higher cardiac output, fluid retention, raised renin), producing tachyphylaxis. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which effect on blood lipids has been reported with prazosin?","choices":["A rise in triglycerides","A mild rise in HDL","A rise in LDL","A large fall in HDL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prazosin shows evidence of a mild reduction in LDL (low-density lipoprotein) and triglycerides and increased HDL (high-density lipoprotein). — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Why is clonidine often given with a diuretic?","choices":["It causes hyperkalemia","It raises renin sharply","It causes rebound edema","It causes sodium retention"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine causes sodium retention, so it is often given with a diuretic; it also decreases antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin) secretion. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"How is the reflex tachycardia seen with prazosin described?","choices":["Marked, like minoxidil","Absent altogether","Severe and dose limiting","Mild"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prazosin causes only mild reflex tachycardia, possibly because of some central sympatholytic activity and because it dilates both arteries and veins. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What should a patient starting hydralazine be told about stool color?","choices":["Stools may turn orange","Stools may turn pale","Stools may turn black","Stools will not change"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stools may turn black on hydralazine, so the patient is warned ahead of time rather than being alarmed by it. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which of these drugs is an alpha-1 blocker?","choices":["Doxazosin","Diltiazem","Guanfacine","Hydralazine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The -zosin suffix marks the alpha-1 selective antagonists: prazosin, terazosin and doxazosin, with tamsulosin as the alpha-1A selective agent. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Clonidine has analgesic activity. What additional use does this support?","choices":["Blunting opiate withdrawal","Migraine prophylaxis","Prostatic hyperplasia","Essential tremor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine's analgesic activity blunts opiate withdrawal reactions. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"When is thiocyanate toxicity most likely during nitroprusside therapy?","choices":["The first minutes of infusion","Only in slow acetylators","Long infusions or renal failure","Only after oral dosing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Thiocyanate accumulates with long-term infusions or renal failure, causing weakness, anoxia, tinnitus, muscle spasms and toxic psychosis. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which is listed as an advantage of central sympatholytics?","choices":["No sedation at all","No withdrawal reactions","No negative effect on lipids","A wide therapeutic range"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Advantages include no negative effects on lipids, efficacy independent of age, race and gender, suitability for monotherapy, and good effect in the elderly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"In which patient is hydralazine contraindicated?","choices":["A young adult with severe hypertension","A patient already taking a diuretic","A patient already on a beta blocker","An older adult with coronary disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hydralazine is contraindicated in coronary artery disease, the elderly and ischemia, because vasodilation with reflex sympathetic activation raises cardiac workload. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"How does prazosin lower blood pressure?","choices":["Stimulates central alpha-2 receptors","Blocks vascular alpha-1 receptors","Opens arteriolar potassium channels","Blocks cardiac beta-1 receptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Reversible blockade of vascular alpha-1 receptors dilates precapillary arterioles and lowers total peripheral resistance, with a reflex rise in heart rate. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A patient using topical minoxidil (Rogaine) for hair growth asks whether it can affect the heart. What is accurate?","choices":["It may have cardiovascular effects","It cannot reach the circulation","It only acts on scalp arteries","It lowers blood pressure reliably"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The topical form is used for hair growth, and it may have cardiovascular effects, so the question is a fair one. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A patient on clonidine runs out of tablets over a long weekend. What is the main danger?","choices":["Rebound hypertension","Profound bradycardia","Prolonged hypoglycemia","Marked hyperkalemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Abrupt withdrawal hypertension is a class side effect, and clonidine withdrawal reactions may be severe, so the drug must not be stopped suddenly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has benign prostatic hyperplasia and hypertension. Which single drug treats both?","choices":["Tamsulosin","Clonidine","Amlodipine","Terazosin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The other alpha-1 selective agents, terazosin and doxazosin, are used both as antihypertensives and for benign prostatic hyperplasia. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which vessels does nitroprusside dilate?","choices":["Arterioles only","Veins and arterioles","Veins only","Coronary arteries only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nitric oxide released from nitroprusside activates cyclic GMP and lowers intracellular calcium in both veins and arterioles. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which statement about hydralazine's mechanism of action is accurate?","choices":["It opens potassium channels","It releases nitric oxide and cyanide","It is not fully understood","It blocks alpha-1 receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The mechanism is not clear; proposed actions include increased cyclic GMP (via nitric oxide), renal prostaglandins and interference with calcium movement. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 100"}]};
